{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe upward bias: The model consistently overestimates prices by 5-10 EUR when the true market is in a downtrend or consolidation phase (e.g., forecasts ~89-90 vs actuals dropping to 78-83). Apply a downward adjustment of 3-5 EUR to the base forecast when recent history shows a peak followed by a decline.",
    "Account for significant lag in trend reversal: The model fails to anticipate the sharp V-shaped recovery (spike from ~78 to ~94) occurring around day 15-20. When sentiment history shifts from neutral/low to positive (0.20) after a prolonged dip, increase the forecast slope for the 15-25 day horizon to capture the delayed rebound.",
    "Adjust for overshoot after volatility spikes: Following the peak recovery (days 20-23), the model forecasts a steady plateau (~90-91), but actuals show a sharp drop and volatility (dropping to 86-87). Reduce the forecast by 2-4 EUR in the final 5-7 days of the horizon if the 20-day mark shows a peak followed by a decline.",
    "Sentiment-weighted mean reversion: When sentiment history is flat (0.00) for extended periods, the model's linear extrapolation is too optimistic. Apply a mean-reversion factor pulling the forecast toward the 18-day historical average rather than the immediate previous day's price."
  ]
}
